1. Narrow vs wide transformation:
    👉Narrow - transformation doesn't inculdes multiple partitions while execution
    👉wide - transformation does inculdes multiple partitions while execution

2. groupByKey vs reduceByKey
    👉groupByKey - group all the values of key and then return -- (key, list of values)
    Eg:
    data = [("A",1), ("A",2), ("B",3), ("B",4)]   
    rdd.groupByKey()
    output:
    ("A", [1,2])
    ("B", [3,4])

    👉reduceByKey- comdine all the values(like sum) return aggregated value for each key
    Eg:
    data = [("A",1), ("A",2), ("B",3), ("B",4)]   
    rdd.reduceByKey(lambda x,y: x+y)
    output:
    ("A", 3)
    ("B", 7)

    🔹 Internal Behavior (Important)
    groupByKey        
    Shuffle all data first → then group
    reduceByKey
    Combine locally → then shuffle → then final combine
    
    🔹 One-line interview answer
    reduceByKey is preferred over groupByKey because it performs local aggregation before shuffle, reducing data transfer and improving performance.

3. map vs flatMap:
Both are transformations, but the difference is in what they return per input element
    👉map - Takes one input → gives exactly one output
    eg:
    data = ["hello world", "spark"]
    rdd.map(lambda x: x.split(" "))
    🔹 Output
    [["hello","world"], ["spark"]]

    👉flatMap - Takes one input → can give multiple outputs Also flattens the result
    eg:
    data = ["hello world", "spark"]
    rdd.flatMap(lambda x: x.split(" "))
    🔹 Output
    ["hello","world","spark"]

4. repartition vs coalesce - 
    👉 repartition
    Used to increase or decrease the number of partitions
    Performs a full shuffle of data across the cluster
    Ensures even data distribution

    ✔ Use when:
    You need better parallelism
    Data is skewed
    Increasing partitions

    👉 coalesce
    Used to reduce the number of partitions
    Minimizes shuffle (tries to avoid it)
    Merges partitions within existing executors

    ✔ Use when:
    Reducing partitions
    Writing output (e.g., fewer files)
    Data is already well distributed

5. cache vs persist
    👉 cache
    Stores DataFrame in memory (RAM) by default
    Shortcut for: persist(MEMORY_ONLY)
    Used when data is reused multiple times
    ✔ Fast but limited by memory

    👉 persist
    Allows choosing storage level:
    MEMORY_ONLY
    MEMORY_AND_DISK
    DISK_ONLY

    ✔ Used when:
    Data is too large for memory
    Need flexibility in storage

6. orderBy vs sortBy:
    👉 orderBy: Sorts a DataFrame by one or more columns and Works on structured data
    df.orderBy(df["salary"].desc())
    ✔ Features
    Can sort by multiple columns
    Uses Spark SQL optimization
    Same as sort()

    👉 sortBy: Sorts an RDD based on a function and Works on unstructured data
    rdd.sortBy(lambda x: x[1])
    ✔ Features
    Custom sorting logic
    Works with key-value or complex objects

    | Feature      | orderBy                | sortBy               |
    | ------------ | --------------------   | -------------------- |
    | API          | DataFrame              | RDD                  |
    | Input        | Columns                | Function (lambda)    |
    | Optimization | Catalyst optimized ✅  | Less optimized ❌   |
    | Usage        | Most common            | Rare (RDD use cases) |

7. RDD vs DataFrame vs Dataset
These are the three ways to work with data in Apache Spark
Think of evolution like this:
RDD → DataFrame → Dataset (more optimized & structured)

    👉 RDD (Resilient Distributed Dataset) - Low-level distributed collection of objects and Works with unstructured data
    ✔ Features
    Full control
    Functional programming (map, flatMap)
    No schema
    ❌ Drawbacks
    No optimization
    Slower performance
    Hard to use
    how it look
        rdd = spark.sparkContext.parallelize([1, 2, 3, 4])
        RDD
        ├── Partition 1 → [1, 2]
        └── Partition 2 → [3, 4]

    👉 DataFrame - Distributed table with columns and schema Like a SQL table
    ✔ Features
    Schema (structured data)
    Uses Catalyst optimizer + Tungsten engine
    Faster than RDD
    ❌ Drawbacks
    No compile-time type safety
    Less control than RDD

    👉 Dataset - Combination of RDD + DataFrame and Type-safe API (mainly in Scala/Java)
    eg(scala):
    case class Person(id:Int, name:String)
    val ds = spark.createDataset(Seq(Person(1,"Arun")))
    ✔ Features
    Type safety [Type safety means errors related to data types are caught early (before or during execution)]
    Optimized like DataFrame
    Better for complex logic
    ❌ Drawbacks
    Not fully supported in Python ❌
    More complex

    | Feature      | RDD  | DataFrame | Dataset    |
    | ------------ | ---- | --------- | ---------- |
    | Level        | Low  | High      | High       |
    | Schema       | ❌   | ✅       | ✅         |
    | Performance  | Slow | Fast      | Fast       |
    | Optimization | ❌   | ✅       | ✅         |
    | Type Safety  | ❌   | ❌       | ✅         |
    | Language     | All  | All       | Scala/Java |

8. left join vs inner join
    👉 LEFT JOIN
    Returns all records from the left table
    Returns matching records from the right table
    If no match → NULL values for right table columns

    ✔ Used when:
    Left table is your main dataset
    You don’t want to lose any records

    👉 INNER JOIN
    Returns only matching records from both tables
    ✔ Used when:
    You only need valid/common data

9. join and broadcast join
    👉 Normal JOIN
    Spark performs a shuffle join
    Data from both tables is redistributed across nodes
    Can be slow for large datasets

    👉 BROADCAST JOIN
    Used when:
    One table is small
    Other table is large

    What happens:
    Small table is broadcasted (copied) to all worker nodes
    Large table stays where it is
    No shuffle required ✅

10. distinct vs dropDuplicate
    👉 distinct - distinct removes duplicate rows based on all columns
    df.distinct()
    👉 dropDuplicate - Removes duplicates based on specific columns (subset)
    df.dropDuplicates(["id"])

11. union vs unionByName
    👉 union - Combines DataFrames based on column position (order)
    👉 unionByName - Combines DataFrames based on column names

    for eg - 
    🔹 Example
    inputs
          df1
        id	name
        1	Arun
        2   mohan

            df2
        name	id
        Ravi	3

    👉 union output - df1.union(df2)
    | id   | name |
    | ---- | ---- |
    | 1    | Arun |
    | 2    | mohan|
    | Ravi | 3    |

    👉 unionByName output - df1.unionByName(df2)
    | id | name |
    | -- | ---- |
    | 1  | Arun |
    | 2  | mohan|
    | 3  | Ravi |

12. filter vs where
They are exactly the same ✅ — just different names
    👉 filter() - Filters rows based on a condition
    df.filter(df["salary"] > 50000)
    👉 where() - Same as filter() (just SQL-style naming)
    df.where(df["salary"] > 50000)

    🔹 Easy Analogy
    👉 filter = Python style
    👉 where = SQL style

13. collect vs take 
    collect() - Brings ALL data from all partitions to the driver
    df.collect()
    ❌ Problem
    Can cause Out Of Memory (OOM) if data is large
    Not safe for big datasets

    take(n) - Returns first n elements only
    df.take(5)
    ✔ Advantage
    Safer than collect
    Reads only required data
    Faster for preview

14. count vs approx_count_distinct
    👉 count - Returns total number of rows [Counts all rows Includes duplicates]
    df.count()

    👉 approx_count_distinct - Returns approximate count of unique values [Counts unique values - Not exact (small error possible)]
    from pyspark.sql.functions import approx_count_distinct
    df.select(approx_count_distinct("id"))

15. explode vs posexplode
    👉 explode - Converts each element into a separate row which doesn't include index
    👉 posexplode - same as explode ALSO give position(index) for the element

    eg:
    Input:
    id	skills
    1	["Python","SQL"]    
    
    👉 explode output:
    from pyspark.sql.functions import explode
    df.select("id", explode("skills").alias("skill"))
    | id | skill  |
    | -- | ------ |
    | 1  | Python |
    | 1  | SQL    |

    👉 posexplode output
    from pyspark.sql.functions import posexplode
    df.select("id", posexplode("skills").alias("pos", "skill"))
    | id | pos | skill  |
    | -- | --- | ------ |
    | 1  | 0   | Python |
    | 1  | 1   | SQL    |

16. window function vs groupBy aggregation
    👉 groupBY- 
    What it does:
    Groups rows
    Returns one row per group
    Other row-level details are lost

    👉 window function
    What it does:
    Performs aggregation without collapsing rows
    Keeps all original rows

    🔹 Example
    Input:
        | dept | name  | salary |
        | ---- | ----- | ------ |
        | IT   | Arun  | 50000  |
        | IT   | Ravi  | 60000  |
        | HR   | Meena | 40000  |

    👉 groupBY output:
    df.groupBy("dept").avg("salary")
        | dept | avg_salary |
        | ---- | ---------- |
        | IT   | 55000      |
        | HR   | 40000      |

    👉 Window function output:
    from pyspark.sql.window import Window
    from pyspark.sql.functions import avg
    windowSpec = Window.partitionBy("dept")
    df.withColumn("avg_salary", avg("salary").over(windowSpec))
    | dept | name  | salary | avg_salary |
    | ---- | ----- | ------ | ---------- |
    | IT   | Arun  | 50000  | 55000      |
    | IT   | Ravi  | 60000  | 55000      |
    | HR   | Meena | 40000  | 40000      |

17. repartitionByRange vs hash partitioning
    👉 repartitionByRange - Redistributes data into partitions based on column ranges
    df.repartitionByRange(3, "salary") -- Distributes data based on value ranges
    Partition 1 → salary < 30k  
    Partition 2 → 30k–60k  
    Partition 3 → > 60k  
        ✔ Use cases
        ORDER BY optimization
        Range filters
        Window functions

    👉 hash partitioning - Uses a hash function on column values and Assigns rows to partitions based on hash value
    df.repartition(3, "id")
    Internally:
    partition = hash(id) % 3
    ✔ Features
    Fast
    Uniform distribution (mostly)
    No ordering guarantee
        ✔ Use cases
        Joins
        GroupBy

18. checkpoint vs cache
    👉 cache - keep data in RAM for quick reuse

    👉 checkpoint - if we have long process it will create a checkpoint in each stages, if we have failure instead of starting from stage 1 it will start process from the last stage where it failed.
    the main purpose is fault torence [handling failure without stopping process]
    spark.sparkContext.setCheckpointDir("/tmp/checkpoint")
    df.checkpoint()
    ✔ Use when:
    Very long DAG
    Risk of recomputation failure
    Complex pipelines
    ❗ Important
    Slower than cache (disk I/O)
    More reliable

    🔹 Easy Analogy
    cache = keep data in RAM for quick reuse ⚡
    checkpoint = save progress permanently 💾

    | Feature     | cache()    | checkpoint()    |
    | ----------- | ---------- | --------------- |
    | Storage     | Memory     | Disk            |
    | Purpose     | Speed      | Fault tolerance |
    | Lineage     | Maintained | Broken          |
    | Recompute   | Possible   | Not needed      |
    | Performance | Faster     | Slower          |


